from _gold_helpers import add, write
A="ALL"
# ---- THE 1744 CODE (pp. 66-69) ----
add(1,"The size of a team is not stated, though it is known that the Grand Matches of the period were played with eleven players on each side.","","1884","1744","A",page=66)
add(2,"No “fresh men” were allowed to act as substitutes on any account.","","23","1744","A",page=66)
add(3,"As to-day, the umpires could be changed during a match, but only by agreement.","","24","1744","A",page=66)
add(4,"Scoring was by “notches” cut on a stick, and “runs” were so termed until c. 1811, though the word “notch” did not finally disappear from the Laws until 1823.","","54","1744","A",bold="and “runs” were so termed until c. 1811, though the word “notch” did not finally disappear from the Laws until 1823.",page=66)
add(5,"The Ball, weighing between 5 and 6 ounces, was to be marked by the umpires so that it could not be changed during a match.","","25; 47","1744","B",page=66)  # 25 = weight clause, 47 = marking/change clause
add(6,"There was no restriction on the width or length of the Bat.","","22","1744","B",page=66)
add(7,"The Pitch was selected by the side which won the toss; this also determined the choice of innings. (Vide Serial Number 11.)","","27","1744","B",page=66)
add(8,"The Wickets, 22 yards apart, consisted each of two stumps and measured 22 by 6 inches, with one bail 6 inches long.","","34","1744","B",page=66)
add(9,"No limit of length was laid down for the Bowling Crease, and the Popping Crease was 3 feet 10 inches from the wicket.","","28; 60","1744","B",page=66)  # 28 = bowling crease, 60 = popping crease
add(10,"No special rules existed, and there is no mention of any provision for rolling, cutting or covering the pitch during a match.","","40","1744","C",page=67)
add(11,"The choice of innings as at present depended on the “Toss of a Piece of Money.” The number of innings was not stated but in the Grand Matches it was usual for each side to have two innings.","","27","1744","D",page=67)
add(12,"The intervals allowed after a batsman was dismissed, and between innings were the same as at present. The umpires called “Play” three times after which refusal to play was “at the Peril of giving the Game.”","","29","1744","D",page=67)
add("","Note to Serial Numbers 8 and 9: prior to 1700 the form of the wicket is doubtful; Nyren (quoting a MS. lent by William Ward) describes a hole cut between the stumps into which the striker had to pop his bat when running a notch, the wicket-keeper having to place the ball in it — origin of the term “Popping”; Haygarth and Pycroft support this and two oil paintings in the M.C.C. Collection show it; Haygarth's suggestion that it lasted to the late eighteenth century has no substance; Goldwin's 1706 description and the 1727 Richmond Articles show the popping hole had disappeared much earlier; touching the umpire's stick appears to have been the forerunner of the Popping Crease.",page=67,row_type="note")
add(13,"No mention is made of how notches were made. The umpires adjudicated on good or bad runs, and this presumably included the case of a short run. If the fielding side obstructed a run, the umpires could order a notch to be scored as a penalty.","","30; 41; 51","1744",bold="The umpires adjudicated on good or bad runs, and this presumably included the case of a short run.",sub="a",page=68)
add(13,"There is no reference to Boundaries.","","1884","1744",sub="b",page=68)
add(13,"There is no reference to “Lost ball.”","","51","1744",sub="c",page=68)
add(14,"The “Over” was a four-ball one at the end of which the umpire called “Over.”","","31; 108","1744",sub="a",page=68)
add(14,"The bowler was allowed to change ends once only in any innings.","","101","1744",sub="b",page=68)
add(15,"While the term “Dead ball” is not used, the meaning is implicit in the Laws.","","49","1744",page=68)
add(16,"A “No ball” was only called when the bowler's “hinder foot” was in front of the bowling crease. It entailed no penalty, and it would seem that the ball was regarded as dead so that the striker could not score off it.","","54","1744",bold="it would seem that the ball was regarded as dead so that the striker could not score off it.",page=68)
add(17,"“Byes” and possibly “Leg Byes” are implied though not explicitly mentioned.","","56","1744",sub="a",page=68)
add(17,"“Wides” were not recognised.","","56","1744",sub="b",page=68)
add(18,"The bail had definitely to fall from the top of the stumps for the wicket to be down—if the bail was already off, then as now, a stump must be struck out of the ground with ball in hand.","","85","1744",page=68)
add(19,"An injured batsman could retire, and claim to resume his innings later.","","96","1744",page=68)
add(20,"Seven of the nine ways in which to-day a batsman can be “Out” have remained unaltered.","","32","1744",sub="a",page=68)
add(20,"“Silver Billy” Beldham is recorded to have stated that he remembered the introduction of the first “L.B.W.” Law as a cure for the “shabby” methods adopted by the batsmen, but the 1744 Laws in fact long previously allowed the umpires to adjudicate on “standing unfair to strike.”","","32","1744",bold="the 1744 Laws in fact long previously allowed the umpires to adjudicate on “standing unfair to strike.”",sub="b",page=69)
add(20,"In the matter of Obstruction, the striker was only out if he left his running ground to prevent a catch; in their running ground either batsman could obstruct a catch, provided they did not use bat or hand for the purpose.","","38","1744",sub="c",page=69)
add(21,"In general the present-day rules applied, but in addition the umpires were to judge of “all frivolous delays, of all Hurts, whether real or pretended, and are discretionally to allow what Time they think proper before the Game goes on again.”","","138","1744","E",sub="a",page=69)
add(21,"As to-day, the umpires could only order a batsman out on appeal. Each umpire at his own wicket adjudicated on all appeals.","","33","1744","E",sub="b",page=69)
add(22,"A committee of the Hambledon Club limited the width of the bat to 4¾ inches in the circumstances already described in Chapter 2. This decision, to be later confirmed by the County representatives, is evidence of the Hambledon prestige at this time—the fact that they settled on a width which has remained unaltered since, does credit to their perception.","6","26","Sept. 25th 1771",bold="A committee of the Hambledon Club limited the width of the bat to 4\u00bc inches in the circumstances already described in Chapter 2.",page=69)
# ---- THE REVISION OF 1774 (pp. 70-72) ----  (sections not labelled in the book after the 1744 code)
d74="Feb. 25th 1774"
add("","The Revision of 1774: carried out by a committee representing Kent, Hampshire, Surrey, Sussex, Middlesex and London — the first occasion on record of any meeting of County representatives. No evidence that the 1755 committee was a standing one, so some changes appearing for the first time may previously have been common practice (known to be so for Serial Number 22).",page=70,row_type="heading",bold=A)
add(23,"Substitutes in the field are no longer definitely prohibited, and it appears probable that they were in fact allowed by consent.","2","45",d74,bold=A,page=70)
add(24,"There is no reference to the umpires being changed by mutual consent.","3","46",d74,bold=A,page=70)
add(25,"The tolerance for the weight of the ball is reduced to be between 5½ and 5¾ ounces.","5","82",d74,page=70)
add(26,"The limit of 4¼ inches for the width of the bat is confirmed.","22","77",d74,page=70)
add(27,"The visiting team has choice of pitch and innings—the pitch to be within 30 yards of a point chosen by the home team. Should the game be played at a “Third Place,” i.e. on a neutral ground, the Bowlers are to toss up for choice of pitch and innings.","7; 11","52; 53",d74,page=70)
add(28,"The Bowling Crease is defined as being 3 feet long on either side of the wicket.","9","65",d74,page=70)
add(29,"The interval between innings is increased from 10 to 15 minutes. The umpires are only required to call “Play” once instead of three times before deciding a match against a side refusing to play.","12","88",d74,page=70)
add(30,"“Short runs” are specially referred to for the first time. (While the Law has remained essentially unaltered, it is of interest to find that a proposal to deduct two runs was considered at the time of the 1835 revision “because such striker, not having run home in the first instance, cannot have started in the second from the proper goal.”)","13","",d74,bold="“Short runs” are specially referred to for the first time.",page=70)
add(31,"The umpires are not directed to call “Over.”","14","79",d74,bold=A,page=71)
add(32,"The “Hit wicket” Law disappears temporarily.","20","37",d74,bold=A,sub="a",page=71)
add(32,"The first specific “L.B.W.” Law is introduced providing for the striker to be out if he “puts his leg before the wicket with a design to stop the ball, and actually prevents the ball from hitting his wicket by it.” This Law clearly placed a very heavy premium on good umpiring, and it is interesting to note the use of the word “leg” instead of “person,” which appeared later.","20","43",d74,sub="b",page=71)
add(33,"The jurisdiction of the umpires for decisions “each at his own wicket” is no longer defined.","21","44",d74,bold=A,page=71)
add(34,"The three-stump wicket is introduced, with one bail 6 inches long. (Vide Chapter 2.)","8","35","c. 1775",page=71)
add(35,"The first reference in an edition of the Laws to the three-stump wicket is found in the Wallis print of this date.","34","36","Sept. 1st 1785",page=71)
add(36,"There is a reference to the use of two bails for each wicket in the Maidstone edition of the Laws.","35","39","c. 1786",bold=A,page=71)
add(37,"The same edition again contains the “Hit wicket” Law.","32","42","c. 1786",bold=A,page=71)  # date inherited from 36
add(38,"The second Maidstone edition includes a new Law dealing with “Obstruction” in identical wording to that later used in the first M.C.C. Code. This reads, “If under pretence of running a Notch, or otherwise, either of the Strikers prevent a Ball from being caught, the Striker of the ball is out.” It might be inferred from this, and from the evidence of Serial Numbers 36 and 37, that a committee carried out some slight revision of the 1774 Code just before the M.C.C. came into being.","20","107","c. 1787",bold="The second Maidstone edition includes a new Law dealing with “Obstruction” in identical wording to that later used in the first M.C.C. Code. [...] It might be inferred from this, and from the evidence of Serial Numbers 36 and 37, that a committee carried out some slight revision of the 1774 Code just before the M.C.C. came into being.",page=72)
# ---- THE FIRST M.C.C. CODE 1788 ----
add("","The First M.C.C. Code—1788: given in Britcher's Scores (1796) as the Code “revised by the Cricket Club at St. Mary-le-bone, May 30th, 1788.” It is not certain that Britcher may not have incorporated changes approved during the intervening years.",page=72,row_type="heading",bold="It is not certain that Britcher may not have incorporated changes approved during the intervening years.")
d88="May 30th 1788"
add(39,"The number of stumps is not specified, and this point is not rectified officially until 1838. (Vide Serial Number 83.) It would seem that the three-stump wicket had been so commonly accepted by this year that no special reference to it was thought necessary. Serial Number 36 is not confirmed, and the bail is specified as being 6 inches long.","36","48",d88,bold="It would seem that the three-stump wicket had been so commonly accepted by this year that no special reference to it was thought necessary.",page=72)
add(40,"For the first time a Law provides for rolling, watering, covering, mowing and beating of the pitch during a match by mutual consent.","10","64",d88,page=72)
add(41,"It is provided that no run shall be scored if the striker be caught, and that the run in which a batsman is run out shall not be reckoned.","13","",d88,page=72)
add(42,"The reintroduction of the “Hit wicket” Law is confirmed in a new form of words.","37","61",d88,page=73)
add(43,"The application of the “L.B.W.” Law is restricted to a ball which has “pitched in a straight line to the wicket.”","32","67",d88,page=73)
add(44,"The jurisdiction of the umpires in respect of an appeal for a catch is again introduced.","33","81",d88,page=73)
d98="1798"
add(45,"The provision of substitutes in the field is recognised without restriction as to where they may field.","23","62",d98,page=73)
add(46,"Permission to change umpires by mutual consent is again granted.","24","76",d98,page=73)
add(47,"A new ball may be demanded by either side at the start of each innings.","5","130",d98,page=73)
add(48,"The wicket is increased to measure 24 by 7 inches, with the bail 7 inches long. (The benefits granted to the bowler by Serial Numbers 47 and 48 are particularly interesting as the first evidence of a set policy to maintain an even balance between the bowler and the batsman—vide Chapter 2.)","39","50",d98,bold="(The benefits granted to the bowler by Serial Numbers 47 and 48 are particularly interesting as the first evidence of a set policy to maintain an even balance between the bowler and the batsman—vide Chapter 2.)",page=73)
add(49,"A new Law provides a penalty of 5 runs if the fieldsman stops a ball with his hat. No doubt the adoption of the top hat as part of the usual attire of the players led to this introduction.","","127",d98,bold="No doubt the adoption of the top hat as part of the usual attire of the players led to this introduction.",sub="a",page=73)
add(49,"The term “Dead ball” is used for the first time in this Law.","15","1884",d98,bold=A,sub="b",page=73)
dB="After 1803 but before May 25th 1809"
add(50,"The bails are referred to in the plural for the first time in a reliable edition of the Laws.","48","59",dB,page=73)
add(51,"A “Lost ball” Law is introduced—the striker to score all the runs he may have made, with a minimum allowance of four.","13","66",dB,page=73)
# ---- p.74 (scanned copy carries handwritten annotations and strike-throughs) ----
ann52_56="Handwritten (inserted 'in Bold against marginal heading'): The changes listed in Serial Numbers 52 to 56, which all appear in the Laws of 1811, may have been made in 1809, as G. B. Buckley has traced in The Morning Herald of June 1st 1809 a notice which reads, 'A great alteration having taken place in the Laws of Cricket, the new Laws to be had of T. Lord.' (M.S. Extracts, Buckley, G.B.—M.C.C. Library.) This extract reinforces the description of the 1811 Liverpool Edition of the Laws given in Appendix III, and its wording suggests more extensive changes than those contained in Serial Numbers 50 and 51 which are derived from the Wallis broadsheet of May 25th 1809 — this with its engraving would have been in preparation some weeks before the start of the cricket season at which time changes in the Laws would normally be approved. The important alterations contained in Serial Numbers 54, 55 and 56 provide the first evidence of the attack, to develop in later years, against the introduction of round-arm bowling, or 'throwing' as it was then called — vide Chapter 2."
dC="Before 1811"
add(52,"The pitch is to be selected by the umpires within 30 yards of a centre selected by the opposing sides.","27","63",dC,annot=ann52_56,page=74)
add(53,"Tossing is again to decide the choice of innings. It would seem from an examination of unofficial codes that as late as 1828 the old rule by which the visiting side had the choice of pitch and innings continued to be used in the case of Home and Away matches being arranged. The new rule however applied in all other cases, and we can imagine in all the Grand Matches.","27","",dC,page=74)
add(54,"The striker is allowed to play at a “No ball,” and to count any resulting runs—he can only be out by being run out.","16","55; 120",dC,struck="(This change is the first evidence of the attack to develop in the ensuing years against the introduction of round-arm bowling—vide Chapter 2.)",sub="a",page=74)
add(54,"The term “run” instead of “notch” is seen here for the first time—it became general by 1823.","4","",dC,sub="b",page=74)
add(55,"The ball is to be bowled under-hand and delivered with the hand below the elbow.","54","58",dC,struck="As in the case of Serial Number 54, we here have evidence of a serious endeavour to obstruct the advent of “throwing-bowling.”",page=74)
add(56,"A Law for “Wides” appears for the first time—the penalty to be one run entered as a “Bye,” though this term remains undefined. This entry however justifies the assumption made in Serial Number 17 that “Byes” had always been a recognised means of scoring.","17","71; 105",dC,page=74)
# ---- p.75 ----
add(57,"A fresh bowler is allowed two trial balls, but having taken them, he must then complete the four-ball over.","","79","Before 1817",bold=A,page=75)
add(58,"The “No ball” Law was again modified with the object of curbing round-arm bowling. The resulting Law was so complicated that in fact it materially assisted the sponsors of the “march of intellect” in their campaign. (Vide Chapter 2.)","55","70","Before 1817",bold="The “No ball” Law was again modified with the object of curbing round-arm bowling. The resulting Law was so complicated that in fact it materially assisted the sponsors of the “march of intellect” in their campaign.",page=75)
add("","The changes listed in Serial Numbers 59 to 61 were almost certainly made in 1819, as G. B. Buckley has traced a Press notice of a match played in Hampshire on June 29th of that year, described as being the first “played out of London with the wicket two inches higher than usual.”",date="Before 1821",bold=A,annot="Handwritten: In September of the same year The Leeds' Mercury referred to the changes as ones which seem to be generally adopted.",page=75,row_type="note")
d21="Before 1821"
add(59,"The Wicket is increased to measure 26 by 7 inches.","50","68",d21,bold=A,page=75)
add(60,"The distance of the Popping crease from the wicket is increased to 4 feet as a result of the increase in the height of the wicket.","9","",d21,bold=A,page=75)
add(61,"The striker is out “Hit wicket” if any part of his dress breaks the wicket.","42","",d21,bold=A,page=75)
d23="Before May 1823"
add(62,"Substitutes are not to bowl, keep wicket, field at point, middle wicket or long stop (to a fast bowler), except by agreement. A direction that the umpires must be punctilious in enforcing this Law was omitted in 1827, but was restored by 1830.","45","75",d23,bold=A,page=75)
add(63,"The selection of the pitch is left entirely to the umpires, i.e. the opponents need no longer select a centre within 30 yards of which the wickets would be pitched by the umpires.","52; 53","1947",d23,bold=A,page=75)
# ---- p.76 ---- (64-67 carry no date of their own in the printed column; inherited from 62-63)
add(64,"After rain the pitch may be changed by mutual consent.","40","73",d23,bold=A,page=76)
add(65,"The Bowling crease is increased to 6 feet 7 inches with Return creases at each end of it at right angles.","28","69",d23,bold=A,page=76)
add(66,"The allowance for “Lost ball” is increased from four to six runs.","51","",d23,bold=A,page=76)
add(67,"The “L.B.W.” Law is altered to require the ball to have been delivered in a straight line to the wicket, and to have struck any part of the batsman's person. This alteration was certainly designed to assist the bowler. As the 1788 rule required that the ball should pitch straight, the first clause must have encouraged the round-arm supporters, and its inclusion appears to have been contrary to the M.C.C.'s current policy (vide Chapter 2).","43","87",d23,bold=A,page=76)
add(68,"The Wicket is increased to measure 27 by 8 inches, with the stumps of sufficient thickness to prevent the ball from passing through, and with bails 8 inches long. This change is almost certainly that mentioned in Bell's Life of June 27th, 1823, as “newly adopted.” (Vide G. B. Buckley, Fresh Light on Pre-Victorian Cricket.)","59","83","After May 1823 but before 1825",bold=A,page=76)
add(69,"The Bowling crease is increased to 6 feet 8 inches to conform with the width of the wicket.","65","117","After 1825 but before 1828",bold=A,page=76)
add(70,"As a sequel to the experimental round-arm matches played in 1827 already discussed in Chapter 2, the “No ball” Law was adopted in a simplified form leaving it crystal clear that the bowler must keep his hand and arm below the elbow.","58","72","May 19th 1828",bold=A,page=76)

